# Nothing to grant here, and the reason is worth keeping.
#
# Android 10 stopped letting most processes open /dev/ashmem and put a
# service in front of it that hands out a descriptor instead. libcutils asks
# for that service first, so the allocator asks too, and is denied finding
# it -- seventy times over a boot.
#
# The rule for it cannot be written: ashmem_device_service is a framework
# type and vendor policy cannot name it. That is the treble split doing its
# job rather than an omission. A vendor process is not meant to reach the
# framework's servicemanager at all, and libcutils knows it -- when the
# lookup fails it opens the device, which the allocator is allowed to do.
#
# So the denials are the fallback working as designed, and the file exists
# so that the next person counting them does not write the rule and find out
# the hard way.
